# Health Literacy -- Plain Language Standards

UK average reading age is 9-11 years. Half of adults struggle with health information. All text that a patient or non-specialist might read must meet these standards.

## Scope

Applies to: UI labels, error messages, tooltips, AI-generated interpretations, executive summaries, report narrative, alert descriptions, and any LLM prompt that produces patient-readable output.

Does NOT apply to: internal logs, code comments, SNOMED codes, or developer-only debug output.

## Writing Rules

1. **Short sentences.** One idea per sentence. Target 15-20 words max.
2. **Everyday words.** Replace clinical jargon with plain English when the audience is non-clinical.
3. **Active voice.** "We found 3 missing codes" not "3 missing codes were identified".
4. **Explain before you name.** Give the plain meaning first, then the clinical term in brackets if needed: "high blood pressure (hypertension)".
5. **No acronyms on first use.** Spell out, then abbreviate: "Quality and Outcomes Framework (QOF)".
6. **Actionable.** Tell the reader what to do or what happens next.

## Common Substitutions

| Avoid               | Prefer                          |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| hypertension         | high blood pressure             |
| prevalence           | how common something is         |
| comorbidity          | other health conditions         |
| contraindicated      | not recommended / not safe      |
| asymptomatic         | no symptoms                     |
| exacerbation         | flare-up / getting worse        |
| aetiology            | cause                           |
| prophylaxis          | prevention                      |
| suboptimal           | could be better                 |
| non-compliance       | not taking medicine as planned  |
| utilisation          | use                             |
| facilitate           | help                            |
| subsequently         | then / after that               |
| aforementioned       | mentioned earlier               |

## LLM Prompt Guidance

When writing system prompts or instructions that produce patient-readable text:
- Include the directive: "Write at a reading age of 9-11 years using plain English."
- Ask the model to "explain medical terms in everyday language on first use".
- Request "short paragraphs of 2-3 sentences".

## UI Text Checklist

- Labels: 1-4 words. Sentence case ("Run pipeline", not "Run Pipeline").
- Tooltips: one sentence explaining why, not just what.
- Errors: say what went wrong, what was not changed, and what to do next.
- Empty states: explain what will appear here and how to trigger it.

## Checking Readability

For critical outputs (executive summaries, interpretation narratives), verify readability:
- Flesch Reading Ease >= 60 (plain English).
- Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level <= 6 (maps to age ~11).
- Free tools: hemingwayapp.com or readable.com.
